SSRF with Blacklist-Based Input Filter
Difficulty: Practitioner
Vulnerability: Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF)
I learned how SSRF can happen when a website does not properly check a URL.
Steps:
Challenges I Faced
While solving this lab, I faced two main problems

 Hostname Blocked
 First, I tried:
http://localhost/admin

The website blocked my request and returned a 400 Bad Request response.
To bypass this hostname filter, I tried:

http://127.1/
It worked because 127.1 is another representation of the loopback address.

 Admin Path Blocked
The website also blocked the /admin path.

I used double URL encoding:
/%2561dmin

This helped me bypass the path filter and access the admin page.

3. Deleting the User

After accessing the admin page, I found the delete endpoint for carlos.

I sent the request through the stock check feature:

http://127.1/%2561dmin/delete?username=carlos

The lab was successfully solved.

Final Result

I learned how weak hostname and path blacklists can allow SSRF attacks against internal resources.

